<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dule Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">984</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dule Temple is a Buddhist temple in Ji County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its Guanyin Pavilion, rebuilt in 984 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, is one of the oldest surviving wooden buildings in China, a two-storey hall built around a 16-metre clay statue of the bodhisattva Guanyin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dule-temple.jpg" alt="Dule Temple" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guanyin_Pavilion_of_Dule_Temple.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Dule Temple, whose name means Temple of Solitary Joy, goes back at least to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, though the year of its founding is unknown and no Tang building survives. In 755 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> held a rally there at the start of his rebellion against the Tang emperor, and the name may refer to him, since he was also known as An Dule, or to the Dule River north-west of the city. The front gate (shanmen) and the Guanyin Pavilion were rebuilt in 984, the second year of the Tonghe era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, by local architects and craftsmen working with Tang techniques.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex faces south. The shanmen is a single-storey gate 10 metres tall, with ridge ornaments (chiwen) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> period, two Liao clay guardian figures, frescoes of the Four Heavenly Kings from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> and a plaque said to be in the hand of the Ming prime minister Yan Song (1480–1567). The Guanyin Pavilion, about 23 metres high and made of more than one thousand pieces, is built around the statue of the Eleven-Headed Guanyin, the largest of its kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, flanked by Liao statues of attendants.</p><p>Rings of columns and bracket sets (dougong) let worshippers see the figure from several levels, and an octagonal caisson covers the open space at its centre. The murals inside were added in a restoration of 1753.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1928 soldiers of the warlord Sun Dianying, who looted the nearby Eastern Qing Tombs, used the main hall as barracks and left bullet holes in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a>, and in 1929 the temple became the Jixian Village Normal School. In the early 1930s it was studied by the Japanese scholar Sadako Sekino and by Liang Sicheng, who in 1966 proposed protecting it with lightning rods, doors and windows. It was listed in 1961 among the first National Priority Protected Sites. The Tangshan earthquake of July 1976 brought down parts of the White Tower, and a survey in 1982 found that the top of the statue had moved about 15 centimetres to the south-west.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dule Temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liao Buddhist architecture survives in some of China's oldest wooden buildings, including the Guanyin Pavilion of <b>Dule Temple</b>, built in 984, and the Sakyamuni Pagoda of Fogong Temple at Yingxian, completed in 1056 and the oldest surviving all-wooden pagoda in China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
